  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\n\nThe central idea is to use a multi-phase learning approach to iteratively refine the step function h. We start with the given initial solution but introduce a new global exploration phase (Phase 0) using self-adaptive particle swarm optimization (PSO) to escape local optima. This is followed by three phases of refinement: a local gradient-based optimization (Phase 1), a ternary-resampling approach (Phase 2), and a final multi-resolution optimization (Phase 3). The current solution likely suffers from local overlap at certain phase shifts, and these phases specifically target this by learning global patterns, enhancing smoothness, and promoting multi-resolution coverage.\n\nKey limitations:\n- Requires parameter tuning for PSO (inertia and cognitive weights)\n- Computationally intensive due to full correlation analysis in each phase\n- Overlap might vary with resolution changes\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\n\n- Representation: Piecewise constant function approximated by samples in [0,1] space with volume preservation.\n- Objective: Minimize max_k `\u222b h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx` by scaling with discretization dx.\n- Constraints:\n  - 0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1 for all x\n  - \u222bh(x) dx = 1 (discretized as sum/points * dx)\n- Formulation:\n  Local search: `\u2207compute_c5(h) + projected gradient`\n  Global optimization: `Pareto-optimal tradeoffs between PSO inertia and system cusps`\n  \n3. Implementation procedure\n\nPhase 0: Global Optimization with PSO\n  - Initialize random feasible function set\n  - Define velocity components: random jumps + fitness-based adjustments\n  - Update step: `h_new = h_best + alpha * (global_best - local_best)`\n\nPhase 1: Local refinement via gradient descent\n  - Use projected gradient steps to minimize overlap\n  - Apply projected clipping to maintain domain constraints\n  - Check against original overlaps by full correlation matrix\n\nPhase 2: Ternary splitting with smoothing\n  - Half: downsample to lower resolution; others: upsample\n  - Redistribute weights by ternary resampling\n  - Recombine best from each path with guided crossover\n\nPhase 3: Multi-resolution polishing\n  - Run 3-5 cycles over all sampling scales\n  - Evaluate overlap integral against increasing resolution\n\nDetailed interface:\n\nPhase0: \n  - Inputs: Initial solution (240 points), target integral 120.0\n  - n_network=40 (PSO particles), max_iter=800, time_check=adaptive\n\n4. Search variations and parameters\n\n- PSO Zone: alpha=0.8, w_range=[0.5,0.9]\n- Phase boundaries: First undecided shift (k0) determines PSO speed\n- Use ternary smoothing cycles (\u03c3=0.75) after each PSO quadrant\n\n5. Budget estimation\n\n- PSO: ~350s @400 \u2192 59%\n- Phase1: Short (~5%)\n- Phase2: Medium (~20%)\n- Phase3: Medium (~15%)\n- Termination: 90% mark for significant improvement\n\n6. Validation\n\n- Final function must meet: 0\u2264h[i]\u22641, integral\u22481\n- Evaluate full correlation (shifts [-n+1,n-1]) with dx=2/(current_n)\n- Score recorded: minimal-overlap value (must meet C<0.380)",
